review_comments:
A simple example would actually involve growing the list.

Today, `AsSpan` returns `0, Count` and so if `Capacity > Count`, it won't cover the entire backing array.

You could of course make this more efficient by avoiding bounds checks and the like, but this is a relatively simple sample showing adding an unknown number of items to a list. It's basically a more efficient version of `AddRange` for when you don't need or want to track `version` changes or repeatedly get the fields to account for potential multithreaded access:
```csharp
int currentCount = list.Count;
int currentCapacity = list.Capacity;

// get the entire backing array
CollectionsMarshal.SetCount(list, currentCapacity);
var span = CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(list);

foreach (var item in items)
{
    if (currentCount < currentCapacity)
    {
        span[currentCount] = item;
    }
    else
    {
        // use the default growth logic
        list.Add(item);

        // get the new backing array
        currentCapacity = list.Capacity;
        CollectionsMarshal.SetCount(list, currentCapacity);

        span = CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(list);
    }

    currentCount++;
}

// shrink down to the actual count of items
CollectionsMarshal.SetCount(list, currentCount);
```

Part of being able to write custom and efficient algorithms really involves getting a span of the full backing array, making your changes, and then updating the minimal associated metadata (most typically `_count`) to accurately reflect those changes
